The assertive/polite decision only considers component test errors (`errorCount`), but `a11yErrorCount` is scoped inside the `if (hasA11yAddon)` block above and is inaccessible here. The PR description states announcements should be `assertive` when component test errors **or** accessibility errors are present, but accessibility errors will always be announced as `polite`. Fix by hoisting `a11yErrorCount` out of the conditional block and including it in the condition:
```ts
let a11yErrorCount = 0;
if (hasA11yAddon) {
  a11yErrorCount = a11yStatusValueToStoryIds['status-value:error'].length;
  // ...push to parts...
}
...
announce(message, errorCount > 0 || a11yErrorCount > 0 ? 'assertive' : 'polite');
```
The existing `AnnouncesTestRunFinished` story doesn't catch this because it exercises the `errorCount > 0` path; a story with only a11y errors would expose the bug.

`@react-aria/live-announcer` populates live-region DOM elements via an internal `setTimeout` (to force screen-reader re-reads). `await userEvent.click(btn)` drains React's microtask queue but does **not** advance macro-task timers, so this assertion runs before the announcement text is written to the DOM and will be flaky. The same issue exists in the `AnnouncesTestRunFinished` play function (line 380) and `AnnouncesTestRunCrashed` (line 414). Wrap each assertion with `waitFor` from `storybook/test`:
```ts
import { expect, fn, userEvent, waitFor } from 'storybook/test';
...
await userEvent.click(btn);
await waitFor(() =>
  expect(document.body).toHaveLiveRegion({ text: 'Test run started.', level: 'polite' })
);
```
